* Dogfood task 2026-07-18 (file-manager follow-up to the earlier device-
* driver dogfood session): minimal custom OS-9/68000 file manager module
* (module type FlMgr=$0D) implementing the 13-entry Create/Open/MakDir/
* ChgDir/Delete/Seek/Read/Write/ReadLn/WriteLn/GetStat/SetStat/Close
* dispatch table documented in os9-systems-dev's file-managers.md.
* Full account: dogfood-report-filemgr-2026-07-18.md.
*
* Purpose: settle whether os9exec's file-manager dispatch (I$ calls go
* to the file manager module first, per file-managers.md) is handled
* differently from device-driver dispatch, which an earlier session
* (dogfood-driver-loopback.a) found os9exec never actually invokes at
* all -- I$Attach is a dummy, and I/O dispatch runs through a hardcoded
* C table (fmgr_op[]) keyed by IO_Type()'s string-prefix path
* classification, never by loading and executing an installed module.
*
* Open (below) writes a known 4-byte magic pattern ('DOFM') into the
* path descriptor's PD_FST working area (offset $2A, per
* file-managers.md's "first 42 bytes are the fmgr/driver-agnostic
* header, PD_FST starts right after") -- if this module is ever really
* invoked, a debugger/dump of a live path descriptor would show it.
*
* VERIFICATION STATUS (see report for full detail):
*   - Assembles clean on r68 (0 errors), links clean on l68 (0 errors);
*     `ident` confirms a genuine, CRC-valid "68000 File Mngr, Object
*     Code, Sharable, System State Process" module (Ty/La=$D01,
*     Attr/Rev=$A001) -- FlMgr=13=$0D was the right module-type code.
*   - CONFIRMED, at both the source and live level, that file-manager
*     dispatch hits the EXACT SAME WALL as driver dispatch, and for
*     the provably SAME underlying reason (not just "similarly
*     broken"): Source/OS9exec_core/icalls.c's OS9_I_OpenCreate (the
*     common body behind I$Open/I$Create) calls IO_Type() exactly
*     once to classify the path into a fixed ptype_typ enum via
*     hardcoded string-prefix matching, then passes that type straight
*     into usrpath_open() -> fmgr_op[type] (filestuff.c's fixed C
*     dispatch table) -- there is no code path anywhere in
*     OS9_I_OpenCreate, IO_Type, or OS9_Device that ever loads a
*     device-descriptor module or reads its M$FMgr field to pick a
*     file manager. Drivers and file managers are dispatched through
*     the literal same function and the literal same table.
*   - Live: loaded this module resident under the name "DOGFM"
*     (confirmed via `mdir`) and paired it with dogfmdesc.a loaded as
*     "DOGFM2" (M$FMgr correctly points at the "dogfm" string). Both
*     `iniz DOGFM2` (silent no-op, `devs` stays empty) and a real test
*     program's `I$Open("/DOGFM2")` (dogfmtest.a; printed
*     "open /DOGFM2 failed") reproduce driver-session's exact failure
*     mode; `dir /DOGFM2` reports the identical error code, Error
*     #000:221 (E$MNF) Module Not Found, that `dir /DOGFOOD` reported
*     in the driver session. No entry point below was ever reached;
*     the PD_FST magic-write in Open was never reachable to test.
* ============================================================

 nam dogfm
 ttl DOGFM custom file manager -- 13-entry Create..Close dispatch table

* ============================================================
* Module-type/attribute constants, hand-defined (module-format.md's
* documented type-code table: FlMgr=13=$0D). A file manager must be
* owned by super-user with the system-state attribute bit set, per
* file-managers.md -- same ReEnt+SuperSt attribute pattern used for
* the earlier Drivr-type module.
* ============================================================
FlMgr    set $0D
Objct    set $01
ReEnt    set $80
SuperSt  set $20
Type_Lang set (FlMgr<<8)+Objct
Attr_Rev  set ((ReEnt+SuperSt)<<8)+1

* PD_FST ($2A): file-manager working area starts right after the
* 42-byte (=$2A) fmgr/driver-agnostic path-descriptor header.
PD_FST   set $2A

* ============================================================
* 13-entry jump table, per file-managers.md's Entry Point Table.
* Bare label references (dc.w Create, not Create-dogfm) -- live-
* verified in the earlier driver session that the linker resolves
* these to true module-start-relative offsets regardless of where
* the psect body itself lands (which is NOT file offset $30 the way
* a single-M$Exec worked example suggests; the assembler
* unconditionally reserves the mod_exec-shaped 12-byte M$Exec/
* M$Excpt/M$Data header before the psect body for every module type).
* ============================================================
 psect dogfm,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,1,0,dogfm

dogfm:
 dc.w Create
 dc.w Open
 dc.w MakDir
 dc.w ChgDir
 dc.w Delete
 dc.w Seek
 dc.w Read
 dc.w Write
 dc.w ReadLn
 dc.w WriteLn
 dc.w GetStat
 dc.w SetStat
 dc.w Close

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Create -- no multi-file/directory support in this minimal file
* manager, so per file-managers.md ("where a file manager has no
* multi-file support, Create is treated as synonymous with Open")
* this just falls through to Open.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Create:
 bra Open

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Open -- (a1)=path descriptor, (a4)=process descriptor,
* (a5)=caller's register stack, (a6)=system globals, per
* file-managers.md's Entry Point Table header. Writes the 'DOFM'
* magic pattern into PD_FST so a live dump can prove this code ran.
* Always succeeds (carry clear).
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Open:
 movem.l d0/a0,-(a7)
 move.l  #$444F464D,PD_FST(a1)   * 'DOFM'
 movem.l (a7)+,d0/a0
 andi    #$FFFE,ccr
 rts

E_UNKSVC set $0216
E_NOTRDY set $0217

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* MakDir -- per file-managers.md, a file manager without directory
* support "simply returns carry set / unknown-service."
* ------------------------------------------------------------
MakDir:
 move.w #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* ChgDir -- no directory structure to search; report unknown-service
* rather than silently pretending to succeed.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
ChgDir:
 move.w #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Delete -- nothing to delete in this in-memory-only manager.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Delete:
 move.w #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Seek -- per file-managers.md, "purely logical... no error if the
* new position is past EOF." This minimal manager has no real file
* pointer to reposition, so it just succeeds unconditionally.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Seek:
 andi #$FFFE,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Read -- returns E$NotRdy (no data ever available from this
* in-memory stub) rather than silently returning zero bytes.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Read:
 move.w #E_NOTRDY,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Write -- accepts and discards the caller's bytes (in-memory-only
* pass-through with nowhere real to put them), reports success.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Write:
 andi #$FFFE,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* ReadLn / WriteLn -- same behavior as Read/Write above; this
* minimal manager applies no line editing.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
ReadLn:
 move.w #E_NOTRDY,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

WriteLn:
 andi #$FFFE,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* GetStat / SetStat -- per file-managers.md's dispatch model this
* should pass unrecognized codes to the driver; this minimal
* manager recognizes nothing and reports E$UnkSvc directly instead
* (no driver dispatch implemented -- out of scope for a dispatch-
* existence probe).
* ------------------------------------------------------------
GetStat:
 move.w #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

SetStat:
 move.w #E_UNKSVC,d1
 ori    #1,ccr
 rts

* ------------------------------------------------------------
* Close -- nothing dynamically allocated by Open, so nothing to
* release.
* ------------------------------------------------------------
Close:
 andi #$FFFE,ccr
 rts

 ends
